WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:01.650
This video is sponsored by Masterworks.

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:04.450
We’ve all heard of the dangers of nuclear
power.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.429
The risk of meltdown; invisible but deadly
radiation; memories of Chernobyl and Fukushima.

00:00:09.429 --> 00:00:13.760
An issue so pervasive, it birthed Die Grünen,
the Green Party, one of Germany’s largest

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:17.610
political forces, with green parties in general
being staunchly anti nuclear.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:20.880
Following the Russian invasion of Ukraine
and the energy crisis however, these positions

00:00:20.880 --> 00:00:22.650
have been increasingly questioned.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:27.340
The two disasters, indeed THE two disasters
most often brought up by anti-nuclear activists,

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:28.859
are Chernobyl and Fukushima.

00:00:28.859 --> 00:00:33.030
These two precedents are used to paint a picture
of the danger nuclear power plants pose.

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:37.739
However, when looking at the facts, those
concerns seem, if nothing else, exaggerated.

00:00:37.739 --> 00:00:38.739
Alarmist, even.

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:39.739
Or do they?

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.460
Is there merit to the criticism, and where
does that put nuclear power in the middle

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:43.460
of an energy crisis?

00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:46.230
About that, power costs have been hitting
historic highs.

00:00:46.230 --> 00:00:49.840
As a result we’ve seen a flurry of government
action to try and rein things in.

00:00:49.840 --> 00:00:53.510
Now, normally people’s income from passive
investments would help ease the pain of higher

00:00:53.510 --> 00:00:54.510
costs of living.

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:58.320
But unfortunately Goldman Sachs reports that
a typical stock-heavy portfolio is expected

00:00:58.320 --> 00:00:58.820
to flatline at best this year.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:04.780
And so what do you do, when inflation is making
your savings a bad place to keep money as well?

00:01:04.780 --> 00:01:06.470
You could put it into investments outside
the stock market.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.240
No, not a safe or a pillow; I’m talking
about real assets, like fine art.

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:14.400
According to Deloitte, 85% of wealth managers
believe there’s an argument for art as part

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:15.640
of a wealth offering.

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:17.702
Even Morgan Stanley specifically talks about
fine art, saying:

00:01:17.702 --> 00:01:22.640
“Investment-grade art has a low correlation
to other major asset classes, and often experiences

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:23.880
lower price volatility.”

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.810
Numbers from CitiBank show that contemporary
art specifically, has a negative -0.04 correlation

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.320
to developed market equities, i.e. major stocks.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.181
Not only can it diversify your portfolio,
it’s had some stellar appreciation, even

00:01:35.181 --> 00:01:39.670
outpacing the S&amp;P 500 for the last 26 years,
by 131 percent.

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:42.360
And during times of crisis, it can actually
appreciate even more.

00:01:42.360 --> 00:01:44.270
Just look at COVID:
contemporary art’s appreciation spiked from

00:01:44.270 --> 00:01:47.840
13.8% for the last 25 years, to a whopping
25%.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.380
No wonder during our current crisis, Masterworks
is seeing increased demand from investors

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:53.920
looking to get contemporary art into their
