00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:37.069
Thank you for checking out Masterworks.

00:02:37.069 --> 00:02:38.819
Ads like these support me creating more content.

00:02:38.819 --> 00:02:41.050
And now, back to the video.

00:02:41.050 --> 00:02:43.990
So THE two examples cited by opponents of
nuclear.

00:02:43.990 --> 00:02:45.890
Let’s take a look at Chernobyl first.

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:46.990
What happened there?

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:51.349
First off, no, the exclusion zone is not STALKER,
there aren’t any gigantic mutated animals

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:53.220
or wondrous radioactive anomalies.

00:02:53.220 --> 00:02:56.440
The zone is in fact one of the best wildlife
sanctuaries on the planet.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.739
There are wild horses, foxes, a whole lot
of animals, happily living in the area.

00:03:00.739 --> 00:03:02.230
Until the Russians arrived, anyway.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:04.360
And the zone itself was at no point abandoned.

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:06.110
Not even the nuclear power plant.

00:03:06.110 --> 00:03:12.360
After Block 4 exploded in 1986, Block 2 went
on operating till 1991, Block 1 till 1996,

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.360
and Block 3 all the way till 2000.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:15.780
But what about the radiation?

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:20.170
To answer this question, I went and asked
Thunderfoot, a PhD researcher and fellow Youtuber.

00:03:20.170 --> 00:03:24.629
He said: Radiation around Chernobyl is comparable
to what you get on an airplane at altitude.

00:03:24.629 --> 00:03:27.530
The plane is more dangerous though, due to
the whole body dose.

00:03:27.530 --> 00:03:30.440
Most of the remaining Chernobyl radiation
is cs137.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:33.340
Cesium in the environment isn't a great danger
unless you eat it.

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.340
So there you have it.

00:03:34.340 --> 00:03:37.769
Chernobyl is generally safe unless you go
around eating the local soil and flora.

00:03:37.769 --> 00:03:40.780
Just 36 years after the world’s worst nuclear
catastrophe.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:44.480
And this is where the alarmism, sensationalism
and people’s general lack of physics knowledge

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:45.480
intersect.

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:47.799
Photos like this might look scary to the Average
Joe.

00:03:47.799 --> 00:03:49.010
Oh, what’s this?

00:03:49.010 --> 00:03:53.299
A Chernobyl mushroom with one of those Genghis
counters next to it.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:54.299
21.88?

00:03:54.299 --> 00:03:56.000
That sounds mighty high, whatever that is!

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.040
Nuclear power is bad.

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:00.030
That picture is from Pripyat, the city right
next to the power plant.

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:04.590
The display shows 21.88 microsieverts, which
equals 0.02 millisieverts.

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:09.019
Which is about one fourth of the dosage you
get… on a transatlantic flight, which is

00:04:09.019 --> 00:04:10.200
0.08.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:16.190
For the sake of perspective, a likely lethal
dose would be 5000 millisieverts, 62500 times

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:20.919
the radiation on transatlantic flights, and
250 000 times that of a Pripyat mushroom.

00:04:20.919 --> 00:04:23.360
It’s not so scary when you put it that way,
is it?

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.560
The destruction of Chernobyl Block 4 itself
is also a special case which nowadays couldn’t

00:04:27.560 --> 00:04:29.169
happen in any developed country.

00:04:29.169 --> 00:04:33.661
Very briefly: the Chernobyl plant was running
Soviet RBMK-1000 reactors, which were cheap

00:04:33.661 --> 00:04:37.500
and easy to build and maintain, but this also
entailed design flaws which were only corrected

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:38.970
after the disaster.

00:04:38.970 --> 00:04:40.280
Basically the thing was very peculiar.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.360
And in order to avoid a runaway thermal feedback
loop, and potentially a catastrophe, you need

00:04:44.360 --> 00:04:46.760
reliable equipment, and highly trained personnel.

00:04:46.760 --> 00:04:48.729
Neither of this was present in Chernobyl.

00:04:48.729 --> 00:04:52.350
On top of that, the Russian Empire, back then
calling itself the Soviet Union, has always

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:56.820
been notorious for cheaping out, and suppressing
inconvenient truths, such as design flaws

00:04:56.820 --> 00:04:58.260
in nuclear reactors.

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:02.690
Or more recently, the actual state of the
Russian Armed Forces.

00:05:02.690 --> 00:05:06.910
Ever since then, all remaining RBMK reactors
have been retrofitted, meaning right now there

00:05:06.910 --> 00:05:10.639
isn’t a single nuclear power plant on the
planet which is able to explode like Chernobyl

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:11.640
reactor 4.

00:05:11.640 --> 00:05:13.530
Fair enough, but what about Fukushima then?

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:14.590
Let’s talk about that.

00:05:14.590 --> 00:05:15.590
The year is 2011.

00:05:15.590 --> 00:05:18.139
It’s the 11th of March, quarter to 15.

00:05:18.139 --> 00:05:22.470
In one minute, the Touhoku Earthquake would
begin, just 72 kilometers off the coast, lasting

00:05:22.470 --> 00:05:23.639
for about six minutes.

00:05:23.639 --> 00:05:26.110
The good news is, it didn’t happen right
under the land.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:30.120
The bad news, it was a magnitude 9 earthquake,
the absolute top of the Richter scale.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.729
As it was underwater, it set off an enormous
tsunami.

00:05:32.729 --> 00:05:36.699
Now, the nuclear power plant was right on
the coast, but behind wave barriers.

00:05:36.699 --> 00:05:38.910
And right up to the tsunami things were in
order.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.850
Of the six reactors, only three were operating.

00:05:40.850 --> 00:05:42.980
The other three were shut down for inspections.

00:05:42.980 --> 00:05:46.669
When the earthquake hit, the three operational
reactors shut down correctly as intended.

00:05:46.669 --> 00:05:50.360
However, coolant has to be circulated even
after a shutdown, otherwise things can heat

00:05:50.360 --> 00:05:52.289
up, and then melt down.

00:05:52.289 --> 00:05:56.130
Since the rectors no longer produced electricity,
emergency diesel generators started running

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:57.229
the coolant pumps.

00:05:57.229 --> 00:06:01.250
However, then the tsunami arrived, so large
that it broke through the wave barriers, and

00:06:01.250 --> 00:06:04.389
flooded the plant’s basement, where those
generators were located.

00:06:04.389 --> 00:06:07.990
All generators failed with the exception of
Reactor 6’s, providing enough power to cool

00:06:07.990 --> 00:06:09.200
both 5 and 6.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:12.280
The first four however were left without coolant,
and melted down.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.849
There were also some hydrogen explosions,
so if you see damage on the plant’s buildings,

00:06:15.849 --> 00:06:16.849
it was that.

00:06:16.849 --> 00:06:20.639
Now you might be asking, how on Earth did
they not think of the tsunami risk, in Japan

00:06:20.639 --> 00:06:23.889
of all places, with a coastal nuclear power
plant of all things?

00:06:23.889 --> 00:06:27.250
Why did they put the generators in the basement,
and how come they didn’t know their wave

00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:29.509
barriers were insufficient for a large tsunami?

00:06:29.509 --> 00:06:31.630
And the answer is, they did know.

00:06:31.630 --> 00:06:35.910
The company operating the plant, TEPCO, has
received warnings about the tsunami risk from:

00:06:35.910 --> 00:06:40.690
the US Nuclear Regulatory Commission, various
Japanese government committees, the Japanese

00:06:40.690 --> 00:06:46.060
Active Fault and Earthquake Research Center,
and two separate in-house studies by TEPCO itself.

00:06:46.060 --> 00:06:49.810
They all came to the same conclusion: if there
is a large enough tsunami, the plant’s existing

00:06:49.810 --> 00:06:53.250
defenses will be wholly insufficient, resulting
in a disaster.

00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:57.490
And TEPCO took all these studies and warnings,
and simply ignored them.

00:06:57.490 --> 00:06:58.490
… Yep.

00:06:58.490 --> 00:07:02.631
If anything, this disaster has shown us how
safe nuclear power plants really are, even

00:07:02.631 --> 00:07:04.440
in such an extreme environment.

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:07.770
Since without decades of gross negligence
and lack of oversight, the plant would have

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:11.509
been fine, even during a magnitude 9 earthquake
and an enormous tsunami.

00:07:11.509 --> 00:07:19.069
The earthquake and tsunami killed 19 759,
injured 6 242, with 2 553 missing.

00:07:19.069 --> 00:07:22.860
So how many people died due to the Fukushima
nuclear disaster?

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:24.030
One.

00:07:24.030 --> 00:07:25.210
… Yeah.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:29.160
And if you look at the plant on a map, you’ll
see that the area around it is very much inhabited.

00:07:29.160 --> 00:07:30.160
People still live there.

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:34.030
There is even a 7-Eleven just 1.5 kilometers
away from the actual reactor blocks.

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.960
As you can see, when you actually look at
the facts, nuclear power, even nuclear disasters

00:07:37.960 --> 00:07:39.520
are suddenly a lot less scary.

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:43.690
However, this didn’t stop Germany for example,
from trying to phase out its nuclear production

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:48.210
from 2011 onwards, which resulted in the country’s
increasing dependence on Russian hydrocarbons,

00:07:48.210 --> 00:07:51.759
which ended up financing the imperialistic,
genocidal conquest of Ukraine.

00:07:51.759 --> 00:07:55.370
There was a parallel attempt at the so-called
Energiewende, a large-scale shift towards

00:07:55.370 --> 00:07:58.990
green energy sources, but that initiative
is now mired in German bureaucracy with no

00:07:58.990 --> 00:08:00.139
solution in sight.

00:08:00.139 --> 00:08:04.400
Thus, in order to plug the hole left by the
nuclear phase-out, Germany had to resort to…

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:05.400
Lignite.

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:09.099
The dirtiest, most polluting, lowest quality
type of coal there is.

00:08:09.099 --> 00:08:13.620
In addition, lignite is mined via open pit
mining, turning the environment into an actual

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.470
wasteland for multiple square kilometers.

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:16.740
So there we go.

00:08:16.740 --> 00:08:20.881
We turned entire regions into wastelands,
to burn the worst possible fossil fuel, just

00:08:20.881 --> 00:08:23.090
so we can shut down those scary nuclear plants.

00:08:23.090 --> 00:08:27.449
Meanwhile, in France, nuclear power constitutes
three-quarters of the energy mix, with six

00:08:27.449 --> 00:08:29.599
new reactors planned in the next 15 years.

00:08:29.599 --> 00:08:34.130
Poland has also opted for nuclear, to replace
their notoriously polluting coal power plants.

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:35.200
And good for them.

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:39.370
There is nothing wrong with nuclear, and opposition
thereof either originates from a lack of knowledge,

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.419
or deliberate spread of sensationalist misinformation.

00:08:41.419 --> 00:08:45.490
You know, it’s easy to report Cesium levels
exceeding the norm a hundred million times,

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:46.600
when the norm is zero.

00:08:46.600 --> 00:08:50.510
And regarding nuclear waste storage, it’s
a pain in the ass, complicated and expensive,

00:08:50.510 --> 00:08:54.610
but by no means unsolvable, and vastly preferable
to dying from the climate catastrophe.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:58.570
Plus turns out the Chernobyl zone is perfect
for large-scale nuclear waste storage.

00:08:58.570 --> 00:09:01.899
Such a facility having been inaugurated a
few months before the invasion.

00:09:01.899 --> 00:09:04.370
Nuclear energy should be the basis of every
modern energy mix.

00:09:04.370 --> 00:09:08.290
It should be used as a stable backbone, with
renewable energy stacked on top.

00:09:08.290 --> 00:09:11.510
This is the surest, most effective way to
getting rid of fossil fuels, and achieving

00:09:11.510 --> 00:09:13.550
carbon neutrality in the energy sector.

00:09:13.550 --> 00:09:17.800
And if you still think nuclear plants are
too risky, global warming is far riskier.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:19.920
So take your pick.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:26.639
Thank you for watching.

